<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
    <string>en</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>squallar</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>Squallar</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Squallar</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
    <!-- `LSMinimumSystemVersion`, not iOS's `MinimumOSVersion`. Kept equal to
         MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET in /.cargo/config.toml, which is what decides
         the Mach-O's LC_BUILD_VERSION; macos/Makefile fails if they differ.
         11.0 is the floor for the instance `-[CLLocationManager
         authorizationStatus]` this app calls. -->
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>$(MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET)</string>
    <!-- Without this AppKit runs the process in 1x scaled mode on a Retina
         display: the wgpu surface is created at half resolution and the map is
         visibly soft. Nothing in the code can compensate for it. -->
    <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Required by App Store Connect's asset validation for a macOS build:
         an upload with no `LSApplicationCategoryType`, or one whose value is
         not a category Apple publishes, is rejected. It is written here and
         not only in the store variant's plist because the key is inert for
         the direct-download build — Finder reads it to group the app in
         "Applications by Category" and nothing else consumes it — so one
         value in one file cannot drift from the other channel's.

         `public.app-category.weather` is Apple's own identifier for the
         Weather category, and is the one whose App Store section the app
         would ship in. -->
    <key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
    <string>public.app-category.weather</string>
    <!-- Both location keys, deliberately.

         `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription` is the iOS key and **does not
         exist on macOS**; Apple's macOS documentation names
         `NSLocationUsageDescription` and says authorization requests "fail
         immediately if the required keys aren't present" — silently, with no
         error at the call site, because `requestWhenInUseAuthorization`
         returns `void`. `objc2-core-location` corroborates it, carrying
         `#[deprecated = "Set the purpose string in Info.plist using key
         NSLocationUsageDescription"]`.

         Shipping only the iOS key is therefore a bug whose entire symptom is
         "the dialog never appears". Shipping both costs one string and is
         correct on every OS version either key has ever been read by. -->
    <key>NSLocationUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Squallar marks where you are and which way you are moving on the weather radar map, and opens the nearest radar site instead of asking you to pick one. Location is read only while the app is open, and every part of the map works without it.</string>
    <key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Squallar marks where you are and which way you are moving on the weather radar map, and opens the nearest radar site instead of asking you to pick one. Location is read only while the app is open, and every part of the map works without it.</string>
    <!-- Export compliance. The reasoning behind `false`, and the list of
         changes that would re-open it, is written out once beside the same key
         in packaging/ios/Info.plist — the two bundles wrap the same Rust
         binary, so the answer cannot differ between them. In short: HTTPS and
         WSS only, `rustls` for the transport with the trust decision handed to
         Apple's Security.framework, no cryptography of this app's own, nothing
         signed, nothing stored encrypted, no encryption feature for the user.

         Carried on the macOS bundle because the macOS App Store asks the same
         question. The notarized Developer ID download does not ask it, and the
         key costs nothing there. -->
    <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
    <false/>
    <!-- Deliberately absent, and each for a reason:

         * `LSRequiresIPhoneOS`, `UIDeviceFamily`, `UILaunchScreen`,
           `UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities`, `UISupportedInterfaceOrientations` —
           iOS keys with no meaning here.
         * `CFBundleSupportedPlatforms`, `DTPlatformName`, `DTSDKName` — Xcode
           build provenance. `simctl` rejects an iOS bundle without the first,
           which is why ios/Info.plist carries it; macOS reads none of them,
           and writing down an SDK name this build never used would be a
           fiction that only drifts.
         * `CFBundleIconFile` — there is no `.icns` in the tree, and inventing
           one from squallar-web/icons is its own change. -->
</dict>
</plist>
